
{"id":"4b0db61d-5dc8-4e6f-b0f3-d02311e8ce94","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002270,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import sys\\n    # Validate top-level keys\\n    required = ('min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent')\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('case must be a dict')\\n    for k in required:\\n        if k not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing required key: {k}')\\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    if not isinstance(min_size, int) or min_size < 0:\\n        raise ValueError('min_cluster_size must be non-negative integer')\\n    clusters = case['clusters']\\n    if not isinstance(clusters, list):\\n        raise ValueError('clusters must be a list')\\n    parent_map = case['parent']\\n    if not isinstance(parent_map, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('parent must be a dict')\\n\\n    # Build all cluster info\\n    all_clusters = {}\\n    for c in clusters:\\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('each cluster must be a dict')\\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('cluster_id must be a string')\\n        sz = c.get('size')\\n        if not isinstance(sz, int) or sz < 0:\\n            raise ValueError('size must be non-negative integer')\\n        bl = c.get('birth_lambda')\\n        if not isinstance(bl, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError('birth_lambda must be a number')\\n        ex = c.get('point_exit_lambdas')\\n        if not isinstance(ex, list):\\n            raise ValueError('point_exit_lambdas must be a list')\\n        if len(ex) != sz:\\n            raise ValueError('point_exit_lambdas length must equal size')\\n        for val in ex:\\n            if not isinstance(val, (int, float)):\\n                raise ValueError('each exit lambda must be a number')\\n        all_clusters[cid] = {'size': sz, 'birth': bl, 'exits': ex, 'children': []}\\n    # Validate parent map: keys must exactly match all cluster ids\\n    expected_keys = set(all_clusters.keys())\\n    actual_keys = set(parent_map.keys())\\n    if actual_keys != expected_keys:\\n        raise ValueError('parent keys do not match cluster ids')\\n    # Build children and check parent values\\n    parent_cache = {}\\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if not isinstance(pid, str) or pid not in all_clusters:\\n                raise ValueError(f'parent {pid} not found for {cid}')\\n            all_clusters[pid]['children'].append(cid)\\n        parent_cache[cid] = pid\\n    # Check for cycles (optional, but good)\\n    # Simple cycle detection: DFS\\n    visited = set()\\n    stack = set()\\n    def has_cycle(node):\\n        visited.add(node)\\n        stack.add(node)\\n        for child in all_clusters[node]['children']:\\n            if child not in visited:\\n                if has_cycle(child):\\n                    return True\\n            elif child in stack:\\n                return True\\n        stack.discard(node)\\n        return False\\n    for cid in all_clusters:\\n        if cid not in visited:\\n            if has_cycle(cid):\\n                raise ValueError('cycle detected in parent tree')\\n    # Filter retained clusters\\n    retained_ids = [cid for cid, info in all_clusters.items() if info['size'] >= min_size]\\n    retained_info = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        info = all_clusters[cid]\\n        exits = info['exits']\\n        birth = info['birth']\\n        stability = sum(max(0.0, ex - birth) for ex in exits)\\n        retained_info[cid] = {'size': info['size'], 'stability': stability}\\n    # Precompute ancestors and descendants for retained clusters\\n    # Ancestors: climb parent links, collect retained ancestors\\n    def get_ancestors(cid):\\n        anc = []\\n        cur = parent_cache.get(cid)\\n        while cur is not None:\\n            if cur in retained_info:\\n                anc.append(cur)\\n            cur = parent_cache.get(cur)\\n        return anc\\n    # Descendants: DFS from cid, only retained (excluding cid itself)\\n    descendants_cache = {}\\n    def get_descendants(cid):\\n        res = []\\n        stack = [cid]\\n        while stack:\\n            node = stack.pop()\\n            for child in all_clusters[node]['children']:\\n                if child in retained_info:\\n                    res.append(child)\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return res\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        descendants_cache[cid] = get_descendants(cid)\\n\\n    # Sort by (-stability, cluster_id)\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda c: (-retained_info[c]['stability'], c))\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    for cid in sorted_ids:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        # check if any descendant already selected\\n        if any(d in selected for d in descendants_cache[cid]):\\n            continue\\n        # select\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        # block ancestors and descendants\\n        for a in get_ancestors(cid):\\n            blocked.add(a)\\n        for d in descendants_cache[cid]:\\n            blocked.add(d)\\n    # Build output\\n    clusters_out = []\\n    for cid in sorted_ids:  # keep original order? Output spec says nothing about order; we can list in sorted retention order\\n        info = retained_info[cid]\\n        clusters_out.append({\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'size': info['size'],\\n            'stability': info['stability']\\n        })\\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected)\\n    return {\\n        'clusters': clusters_out,\\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) that implements condensed tree stability as described. The payload has:\n- min_cluster_size: integer, minimum size for a cluster to be considered.\n- clusters: list of objects with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list).\n- parent: mapping from cluster_id to parent cluster_id or null for root.\n\nWe need to:\n1. Filter out clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n2. For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum over its points of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Note: birth_lambda is per cluster, but exit_lambdas are per point. So for each point in the cluster (size = number of points), we have its exit_lambda from point_exit_lambdas list. The list length should equal size. Compute for each point: max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Sum them.\n3. Rank clusters by decreasing stability, and for ties, by cluster_id (presumably lexicographic? But need to specify: \"cluster ID\" as string, likely ascending). The ranking for selection: we need to select clusters, but condition: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" Descendant means a cluster that is a child (or deeper) of the current cluster. Blocking ancestors means if a cluster is selected, then its ancestors cannot be selected (since they are blocked). This is typical for HDBSCAN condensed tree selection: choose clusters with highest stability, and once chosen, none of its descendants or ancestors can be chosen? Actually typical: choose the most stable clusters, and once you choose a cluster, you exclude its descendants and ancestors. But the description: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That means we consider clusters in order of decreasing stability; for each cluster, if any already selected descendant exists, then this cluster is blocked (cannot be selected). Otherwise, we select it, and then block its ancestors (i.e., mark ancestors as blocked so they cannot be selected later). Also need to handle ties: use cluster ID as secondary sort (ascending). So we need to implement the selection algorithm.\n\nWe also need to return:\n- \"clusters\": array of objects with cluster_id, size, stability (computed). This includes all retained clusters (those with size >= min_cluster_size), even if not selected.\n- \"selected_cluster_ids\": list of cluster_id strings in sorted order? The problem says \"sorted selected IDs\". Likely ascending by cluster_id? Or by stability rank? Output spec for selected_cluster_ids is just an array of strings, no ordering constraint specified. But candidate artifact says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So we should sort selected cluster IDs (presumably ascending string order).\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Validate required fields in payload: min_cluster_size, clusters, parent. clusters must be list, each with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. parent dict keys must match cluster_ids and values are cluster_ids or null. point_exit_lambdas list length must equal size. If any validation fails, raise ValueError.\n- Filter clusters: keep those with size >= min_cluster_size. Build dict of cluster info.\n- For each retained cluster, compute stability as described.\n- Sort clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) for selection order.\n- Build parent-child relationships using parent dict. For selection, we need to know descendants and ancestors. Use tree traversal.\n- Selection algorithm:\n  Initialize selected = set()\n  Initialize blocked = set() (ancestors of selected clusters, plus descendants? Actually we block ancestors after selection; also we check that no already selected descendant blocks it. So we need to avoid selecting a cluster if it has any selected descendant. So we need to track for each cluster, which selected descendants exist? Or we can do: process clusters in order, for current cluster c, if c is blocked (by being ancestor of a selected cluster? Actually the condition is \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". That means if there is any selected cluster that is a descendant of c, then c is blocked. So we need to check if any selected cluster is in the set of descendants of c. Alternatively, we can maintain a set of selected clusters, and for each candidate, check if any selected cluster is descendant. Since the tree is static, we can precompute descendants for each cluster. But we only need to check selected descendants. So we can process in order and maintain a set of selected IDs. For each candidate c, check if any selected ID in descedants(c). If none, then select c, add c to selected, and then block its ancestors (i.e., add all ancestors of c to a blocked set so they cannot be selected later). Actually we need to block ancestors to prevent them from being selected. So we can maintain a blocked set of clusters that are ancestors of selected clusters. In the loop, before checking, we also need to ensure c is not blocked (i.e., not an ancestor of a selected cluster). But the condition is only \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". That is exactly the same as: c is not an ancestor of any selected cluster? Actually if c is ancestor of selected descendant, then that descendant blocks c. So c is blocked by its descendant. So we need to check if c has any selected descendant. That is the same as: c is ancestor of at least one selected cluster. So if c is in the ancestor set of any selected cluster, then c is blocked. So we can maintain a set of blocked ancestors: for each selected cluster, add all its ancestors to blocked. Then for candidate c, if c is in blocked, skip. This ensures that no selected descendant exists (since if it had, c would be in blocked). However, we must also consider that selecting c blocks its ancestors. So after selecting c, we add all ancestors of c to blocked.\n\nBut careful: the algorithm says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So we don't need to explicitly block descendants? Actually selecting a cluster does not block its descendants; descendants are allowed to be selected? No, typical condensed tree selection: once a cluster is selected, no descendant or ancestor can be selected. But the description only says block ancestors after selection. However, if we later consider a descendant of a selected cluster, then the descendant would be considered. But then the selected cluster is an ancestor of the descendant; does that block the descendant? The condition says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". For a descendant, \"already selected descendant\" would be none (since descendant is after ancestor in selection order? order is by stability; likely stable clusters are higher up, so descendants might have lower stability, so they would be considered later. When considering descendant, it will have an ancestor (selected cluster). According to rule, \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" - the descendant is being considered, its descendants may block it, but its ancestors are not considered here. However, after selecting the ancestor, we block ancestors, but not descendants. So descendant could still be selected? That would create a situation where both ancestor and descendant are selected, which is undesirable because selected clusters should be disjoint lineages. The typical HDBSCAN algorithm: process clusters in order of stability; for each cluster, if its parent has not been selected and none of its descendants have been selected, then select it. Then mark its descendants as ineligible. Actually HDBSCAN selects clusters that are \"leaf\" clusters in the condensed tree after stability pruning. The algorithm: compute stability for each cluster, then traverse the tree from leaves upwards, selecting a cluster if the sum of stabilities of its children is less than its own stability; then propagate stability upward. But the description given here is different: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". That means we process in descending stability order; for each cluster, if any of its descendants (which would be processed earlier if they have higher stability) have been selected, then this cluster is blocked. So descendants are processed first if they have higher stability, which is typical: leaves may have higher stability? In HDBSCAN, smaller clusters (leaves) often have higher stability, so they get selected first, then ancestors are blocked. So the algorithm described: start with highest stability cluster, select it, then block its ancestors. Then next cluster: if it's a descendant of a selected cluster? That would have been processed earlier? No, if descendant has higher stability, it would have been processed first. So later we consider a cluster that is an ancestor; its descendant is already selected, so it's blocked. So it works. But if there is a case where a descendant has lower stability than its ancestor, then the ancestor will be processed first. Then the ancestor is selected, and its ancestors blocked. Then when considering the descendant later, the descendant has a selected ancestor; does that block it? The rule only says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". It doesn't say anything about selected ancestors. So the descendant could be selected even though its ancestor is already selected. That would violate typical HDBSCAN (select only independent subtrees). But perhaps the intended algorithm is to also block descendants? Let's read carefully: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" It does not mention blocking descendants. However, typical condensed tree selection (as in HDBSCAN) selects clusters such that no cluster is selected if any of its ancestors or descendants are selected. But they phrased it in terms of descendants blocking ancestors. Perhaps they assume the processing order ensures that if a descendant has lower stability, it will be processed after its ancestor, and then the condition \"no already selected descendant\" is satisfied (since none selected yet), so the descendant would be selected, and then block its ancestors (including the already selected ancestor). But would that override the previous selection? They don't say to unselect. So it seems ambiguous. Let's look at the candidate artifact: \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This implies that the selection order is decreasing stability, so higher stability first. Typically, leaf clusters (descendants) have higher stability, so they will be selected first. Then when we consider an internal cluster (ancestor), it will already have a selected descendant, so it is blocked. That matches HDBSCAN. If a descendant has lower stability, it will be considered later, but then \"no already selected descendant\" - none of its descendants have been selected because they are even lower? Actually if descendant has lower stability, its own descendants might be even lower or may have been processed earlier if they have higher stability. It's messy.\n\nBut looking at the public fixture: clusters c0 (size 7), c1 (size 4), c2 (size 2). min_cluster_size=3, so c2 is dropped (size 2 < 3). So only c0 and c1 retained. parent: c0 root, c1 child of c0. So we need to compute stabilities. For c0: birth_lambda=0.2, point_exit_lambdas=[0.4,0.6,0.9] (3 points, but size=7? Wait size is 7 but point_exit_lambdas has only 3 entries. That is a mismatch! The payload says size=7 but point_exit_lambdas length 3. That's invalid. But the fixture might be just a shape? Actually it's a public fixture for input shapes, but the example should be valid. Let's check: payload has clusters: c0 size 7, but point_exit_lambdas length 3; c1 size 4, point_exit_lambdas length 3; c2 size 2, point_exit_lambdas length 1. So none match size. This is likely intentional to test validation? But the task says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we must validate that point_exit_lambdas length equals size. If not, raise ValueError. So for the given fixtures, all would raise ValueError because sizes don't match. That seems odd. Perhaps the fixture is just for shape, not for actual valid data. The task description says \"The attached public fixtures define input shapes only.\" So they are not necessarily valid. So we should implement validation that raises ValueError for invalid input.\n\nThus for solve, we need to validate: each cluster must have cluster_id, size (int >=0), birth_lambda (number), point_exit_lambdas list of numbers with length equal to size. Also parent dict must have keys exactly the set of cluster_ids, and values must be either null or cluster_id that exists. Also min_cluster_size integer >=0. If any validation fails, raise ValueError.\n\nThen proceed with algorithm. Since the fixtures are invalid, we can't test, but we need to implement correctly.\n\nNow, implementation details:\n- Use standard library: no external libs.\n- Return a dict with 'clusters' list and 'selected_cluster_ids' list.\n- For each retained cluster, compute stability.\n\nFor selection:\n- Build a mapping from cluster_id to its children list.\n- Build ancestor mapping: for each retained cluster, list of ancestors up to root.\n- Compute ancestors for each cluster: we can precompute.\n- Sort retained cluster IDs by (-stability, cluster_id).\n- Initialize selected = set() and blocked = set() (ancestors of selected clusters).\n- For each cluster_id in sorted order:\n  - if cluster_id in blocked: continue (because a selected descendant blocks it? Actually blocked set contains ancestors of selected clusters, so if this cluster is an ancestor of a selected cluster, it's blocked. That's correct.)\n  - else: select it: add cluster_id to selected; add all ancestors (excluding itself) to blocked set.\n- After loop, selected cluster IDs sorted ascending by string.\n- Also, we need to include all retained clusters in output, not just selected.\n\nBut careful: The condition \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means we should check if any descendant of current cluster is already selected. Our blocked set only contains ancestors of selected clusters. So to check if any selected descendant exists, we can check if any of current cluster's descendants is in selected. That's more direct. But building blocked set from ancestors of selected clusters is equivalent: if c is ancestor of any selected cluster, then c is blocked. So we can build blocked by iterating selected clusters and adding ancestors. However, we need to ensure we don't block clusters that are descendants of selected clusters? Not needed because descendants are not blocked by this method, but they could be selected later if they have higher stability? In typical HDBSCAN, once a cluster is selected, its descendants are not selected. But here, if a descendant has higher stability, it would have been processed first and selected. If a descendant has lower stability, it would be processed later; but then it might be selected because its ancestor is selected? That would violate typical semantics. However, the description only mentions blocking ancestors after selection, not descendants. It also says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" – so if a descendant is already selected, it blocks the ancestor. But it doesn't say that a selected ancestor blocks descendants. So theoretically, a descendant could be selected after its ancestor is selected, as long as no descendant of that descendant blocks it. That would result in both selected. Is that intended? Possibly not, but the description is explicit. However, the candidate artifact says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So maybe the algorithm is: process in decreasing stability; to select cluster, it must have no selected descendant (i.e., none of its children/subtree already selected). That's consistent with processing top-down? Actually processing top-down (higher stability first) means descendants might be processed later if they have lower stability. Then when we get to the descendant, its ancestors are already selected. The condition \"no already selected descendant\" is trivially true (none), so it would be selected, then block its ancestors (which already selected). That would create conflict. But they don't say to unselect. So perhaps they expect the ordering to always put leaves first because leaves have highest stability (due to more points? Not necessarily). But we must follow the literal description.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll implement as: process clusters sorted by decreasing stability then cluster_id. For each cluster, if any of its descendants (recursive) is already selected, skip. Else select it, then mark all its ancestors as blocked (so they can't be selected later). That is a common algorithm. But also, after selecting, we should also block its descendants? Not in description. However, note that if we select a cluster, and later a descendant with lower stability is considered, it will have an ancestor selected, but the condition only checks for selected descendants, not ancestors. So it would be selected. To prevent that, we could after selecting a cluster, mark all its descendants as blocked too. But description doesn't say that. I'll stick to the literal: only block ancestors after selection. Then descendant selection is allowed if its descendant is not selected. But then we have both selected. That might be intentional? We need to consider typical HDBSCAN: they don't allow overlapping selected clusters. So likely the intended algorithm is that after selecting a cluster, all its ancestors and descendants are blocked. But the text says only block ancestors. Could be an oversight. Let's see the candidate artifact: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This is exactly the algorithm used in HDBSCAN: you process in order of stability; you select a cluster if none of its descendants have been selected (i.e., it's a \"leaf\" in terms of selected clusters); then you block its ancestors. This ensures that you never have two selected clusters where one is ancestor of the other, because if you select a leaf, its ancestors become blocked. Later, when you consider those ancestors, they are blocked. But if you consider a descendant of a selected cluster, it would have an ancestor selected; but the condition only checks descendants, not ancestors, so it could be selected. But in practice, descendants have higher stability typically, so they would be selected first, making ancestors blocked. If a descendant has lower stability, it would be considered later; but then its ancestor is already selected; the descendant's own descendants (if any) might not be selected, so the condition passes and it gets selected, contradicting disjointness. So to avoid that, we need to also block descendants after selection. Let's examine the HDBSCAN condensed tree algorithm as described in literature: \n- For each cluster, compute stability.\n- Process from leaves to root (or stability order), for each cluster, if its stability > sum of stabilities of its children, then select this cluster and mark its descendants as not selected; otherwise, propagate stability up.\nAlternatively, there is a greedy selection: sort clusters by decreasing stability, then for each cluster, if it is not yet blocked, select it and block all its ancestors and descendants. That ensures disjointness.\n\nGiven the instruction \"block its ancestors\", I lean towards the greedy method that only blocks ancestors. But I suspect they want the standard HDBSCAN selection that results in a flat clustering. However, the candidate artifact says \"stable_ties\" and \"noise_semantics\", and mentions deterministic tie handling using cluster ID. So I'll implement the algorithm that selects clusters in order of decreasing stability, with tie-breaking by cluster ID ascending, and for each cluster, if any descendant is already selected, skip; else select it and block all its ancestors. This is typical and avoids overlapping. To block descendants, we would need to also block descendants after selection. But given text, I'll only block ancestors and check descendants. That should work if processing order ensures descendants are processed before ancestors. To be safe, I'll also block ancestors of the selected cluster (including itself? No, ancestors only). That will prevent ancestors from being selected later. Descendants are not blocked, but they will be processed after (if they have lower stability) and could be selected. But they can only be selected if they have no selected descendant. Since the selected cluster is an ancestor, not a descendant, the descendant will have a selected ancestor, but that doesn't block it. So it would be selected, leading to overlapping. To prevent that, we need to check for selected ancestors as well. The condition should be \"no already selected cluster in its lineage (ancestor or descendant)\". But the text only says descendant. I'll re-read: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". That is one-direction. So I'll follow exactly. But then the output might have clusters that are ancestors of each other selected, which is likely not intended. However, the fixture examples might not cover that. I'll go with the standard greedy selection as used in HDBSCAN: sort by stability descending; for each cluster, if it is not blocked (by being ancestor or descendant of a selected cluster), then select it and block all its ancestors and descendants. That ensures disjoint clusters. The text says \"block its ancestors\" but it doesn't say block descendants. However, the condition for selection is about descendants, not ancestors. So after selection, to prevent a descendant from being selected later, we need to block it. The algorithm then would be: when selecting a cluster, block all ancestors and descendants. That is common. I'll implement that: after selecting, add all ancestors and all descendants to blocked set. But the condition for selection is only \"no already selected descendant\". That means we don't need to check ancestors because they will be blocked later. So: for cluster c in sorted order:\n if c in blocked: continue.\n if any descendant of c is in selected: continue. (descendant selected? but we haven't blocked descendants? Actually if a descendant was selected earlier, then c would have been blocked because we would have added c as ancestor of that descendant? We will add ancestors of selected clusters to blocked. So c would be in blocked already from previous selections. So the check for descendant in selected might be redundant if we always block ancestors. But if we also block descendants after selection, then descendants of selected clusters are blocked and won't be considered later. So I'll implement:\n   if c in blocked: continue (blocked includes ancestors and descendants of selected clusters)\n   else: select c, add c to selected, add all ancestors of c to blocked, add all descendants of c to blocked.\n That ensures no overlapping.\n\nI think that's reasonable and matches typical condensed tree selection.\n\nAlso, after selection, we need to sort selected cluster IDs ascending for output.\n\nLet's write solve(case) accordingly.\n\nValidation steps:\n- Check case is dict, has keys min_cluster_size, clusters, parent.\n- min_cluster_size must be int >=0.\n- clusters must be list.\n- For each cluster dict: must have cluster_id (str), size (int >=0), birth_lambda (int/float), point_exit_lambdas (list of numbers). Ensure len(point_exit_lambdas) == size. If size is 0, point_exit_lambdas should be empty list.\n- parent must be dict with keys exactly the set of cluster_ids from all clusters (including those below min size? Probably all clusters, a